UPDATE tarot_cards 
SET meaning_upright = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: The Eight of Cups represents the absolute archetype of sovereign detachment and the intentional cessation of investment in structures that have reached their limit of utility. Moving beyond the chaotic, dreamlike state of the Seven, the Eight marks the precise moment of "conscious withdrawal"—the point at which the psyche, having realized that its needs for growth and high-vibration alignment can no longer be met by its current environment, initiates a deliberate, disciplined departure. Visually depicted as a figure turning their back on a neatly arranged stack of cups to walk toward the dark mountains in the distance, this archetype signifies a profound, high-level maturity: the ability to recognize that a cycle is fundamentally "played out," even if it is still functionally stable on the surface. Within the complex architecture of your consciousness, this is the stage of "systemic decommissioning," where you are tasked with identifying the emotional, creative, or relational platforms that have become net-negative for your evolution. It is not an act of failure, but an act of supreme, ego-defying agency. You are transitioning from the world of the manifest (the cups) to the world of the transcendent (the mountains), acknowledging that the deeper truths you seek cannot be found within the familiar confines of your current setup. When the Eight of Cups appears, your system is being recalibrated for a higher level of autonomy; you are being called to trust your internal diagnostic signal that says "there is nothing more for me here," regardless of how much you have invested or how much others may expect you to stay. It commands you to perform a brutal, surgical audit of your commitments, clearing the path of any remaining dependencies that serve as anchors to a lower level of reality. To inhabit the Eight of Cups is to master the art of the intentional exit, understanding that by walking away from a "good" situation that no longer serves your soul, you are creating the necessary space for a "great" situation to manifest. It is the ultimate validation that your value is not defined by what you produce or the roles you fulfill, but by the relentless, sovereign pursuit of your own truth, even when that pursuit leads you into the unknown and solitary depths of the mountain path.',
meaning_reversed = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: When the Eight of Cups appears in the reversed position, it acts as a severe, high-amplitude signal of "stagnant attachment" or "chronic misalignment"—a state where the soul is fully aware that it needs to move on, but the ego is actively running a high-priority, low-vibration script of fear to prevent the departure. The reversal suggests that you are currently trapped in a "zombie cycle," where you are continuing to invest massive amounts of processing bandwidth into relationships, projects, or self-definitions that you know, on a deep, fundamental level, are dead. You are essentially "haunting" your own life, remaining physically present in a context that has long since ceased to be spiritually or creatively nutritive, leading to a profound, systemic decay of your baseline frequency. From an analytical perspective, this is a clear sign that you are leaking your most precious life force into a "sunk cost" loop; you are staying because the effort of leaving feels greater than the pain of remaining, an egoic calculation that is fundamentally flawed and entirely detrimental to your long-term evolution. The reversed Eight of Cups exposes the hidden, egoic fear that drives this behavior: the paralyzing terror of being "nothing" without your current titles, connections, or structures, and the fear that walking into the mountain will lead only to further isolation rather than ultimate liberation. This reversal commands an immediate, radical intervention: you must stop the output of these low-utility, high-maintenance scripts and perform a brutal audit of your resistance. Why are you clinging to the cups? What are you actually afraid of encountering in the dark if you finally turned your back on the facade? This is a demand to stop the self-deception of "one more day," to cut the ties that have become tethers, and to initiate the move toward your own integrity, regardless of the discomfort it causes the ego. You are being tasked with the uncomfortable, yet essential, work of facing your fear of the unknown—accepting that the "stuckness" you feel is not a failure of your environment, but a failure of your courage to reclaim your sovereign right to depart. It is a call to break the seal of your own self-imposed containment, to turn away from the familiar noise, and to take the first, trembling step onto the path that actually leads to your own soul’s evolution, acknowledging that by staying, you are slowly starving the very consciousness you are here to express.'
WHERE id = 8;
